{
 "schemaVersion": 1,
 "id": "kukhnya-s01e03-scene-10",
 "pair": "ru-en",
 "title": "История про гуся  (The Story of the Goose)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "barinov": "Шеф",
  "lyova": "Лёва",
  "max": "Макс"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Max has quietly swapped the live goose for a ready-plucked one from the market, and now he has to explain to Barinov why his apron is spotless. He tells the chef a story that is more dramatic than the truth. Here is the Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Огузок! Где мой свежий гусь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я только что прикончил его!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Почему ты такой подозрительно чистый?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "На тебе нет ни капли крови!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я его совсем не резал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "И как ты его убил?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Шеф, я его просто задушил!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Какой кошмар! Настоящий Отелло на кухне!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Тащи свою птицу су-шефу на стол!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Лёва, начинай быстро готовить гуся!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Понял, шеф, я включаю духовку.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А я займусь важным комплиментом.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я спас птицу и свою работу.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now the lie, line by line.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Огузок! Где мой свежий гусь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Oguzok! Where is my fresh goose?' Barinov opens with the nickname. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Где",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the word for \"where\", and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "свежий",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the word for \"fresh\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я только что прикончил его!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I have just finished it off!' Another detail, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "прикончил",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is built from the word for \"end\", so it means \"brought to an end\". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "только что",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally means \"only what\", but translates to \"just now\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Почему ты такой подозрительно чистый?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why are you so suspiciously clean?' Take note of ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "подозрительно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is built from the word for \"suspicious\", changing the ending to make it a how-word. The word for \"clean\" is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "чистый?",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "На тебе нет ни капли крови!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'There is not a drop of blood on you!' You will hear ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "ни",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"not even\". The words for \"drop\" and \"blood\" both take the \"of\" shape.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я его совсем не резал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I did not cut it at all.' In this sentence, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "совсем не",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"not at all\". And the action word for \"cut\", ending with an \"l\" sound for a man, is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "резал.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "И как ты его убил?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And how did you kill it?' Another detail, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "как",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"how\". And built from the same action word we saw in the goose scene is the word for \"killed\", ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "убил?",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Шеф, я его просто задушил!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Chef, I simply strangled it!' Pay attention to ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "просто",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"simply\". And built from the word for \"to choke\", adding a starting sound that means bringing it to an end, is the word for \"strangled\", ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "задушил!",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Какой кошмар! Настоящий Отелло на кухне!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What a nightmare! A real Othello in the kitchen!' The chef is joking because in Shakespeare his wife is strangled by ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Отелло",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ". The phrase for \"what a nightmare!\" translates to ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Какой кошмар!",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Тащи свою птицу су-шефу на стол!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Drag your bird to the sous-chef's table!' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Тащи",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the word for \"drag\", said of anything heavy. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "су-шефу",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"to the sous-chef\", taking a \"to\" ending, and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "свою птицу",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"your own bird\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Лёва, начинай быстро готовить гуся!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Lyova, start preparing the goose quickly!' Let us check ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "начинай",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is an informal order meaning \"start\". Changing the ending on the bird, \"to cook the goose\" translates to ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "готовить гуся!",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Понял, шеф, я включаю духовку.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Understood, Chef, I am turning on the oven.' Take note of ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "включаю",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", the word for \"I switch on\", also translates to \"I include\", and it works for any switch. Built from the word for \"hot air\" is the word for oven, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "духовку.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А я займусь важным комплиментом.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And I will take care of an important compliment.' Take note of ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "займусь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"I'll take care of\". What you take care of changes its shape, so the free little dish becomes an \"important compliment\", or ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "важным комплиментом.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я спас птицу и свою работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I saved the bird and my job.' You will hear ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "спас",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", the word for \"saved\", is related to the word for \"thank you\", which literally means \"may God save you\". \"My own job\" translates to ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "свою работу.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Огузок! Где мой свежий гусь?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я только что прикончил его!",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Почему ты такой подозрительно чистый?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "На тебе нет ни капли крови!",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я его совсем не резал.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "И как ты его убил?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Шеф, я его просто задушил!",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Какой кошмар! Настоящий Отелло на кухне!",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Тащи свою птицу су-шефу на стол!",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Лёва, начинай быстро готовить гуся!",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Понял, шеф, я включаю духовку.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А я займусь важным комплиментом.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я спас птицу и свою работу.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Max has saved the goose, and he has also saved his job. Remember \"not a drop\", which is how they say \"not even a bit\".",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Пока!",
   "gap": 0.3
  }
